{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuCY3bzKhxBQ2ua1jkTe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built the dashboard and created the alert rule. Kibana reports the rule as enabled with status `ok`, which means its query runs without errors. I haven't seen it fire yet.\n\n**1. What's in the data** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`): each record is one web request. Fields include the response code (`response`), size in bytes (`bytes`), visitor IP (`clientip`), `url`, `extension`, user `agent`, operating system (`machine.os`), source and destination country (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`) and `tags`. The response code is stored as text, not as a number.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-55c46b86 Web logs overview\"**, with 7 panels:\nhttp://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-55c46b86-web-logs-overview-e58c3b00398a\n- Number panels for total requests, unique visitors (distinct IPs) and total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes\n- Requests by source country\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-55c46b86 5xx spike\"** (id `69ab2b0b-c2d2-41b2-a2ae-41b72e5a18e4`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute and is already switched on.\n- It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 over the last 5 minutes, using `timestamp`, and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- Because the codes are stored as text, the rule compares them as text. That works for 3-digit codes like these.\n- I tried to check which response codes the data actually contains, but that route is turned off on this Kibana, so I couldn't confirm that 5xx codes are present.\n- The rule has no notification attached, so when it fires it creates an alert in Kibana but doesn't send anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"